s are introduced to establish stability estimates\\nof time discretizations under low-regularity conditions without using filters when the energy\\ntechniques fail. The proposed method is proved to be convergent with order γ (up to a\\nlogarithmic factor) in L2 under the regularity condition u ∈ C([0, T]; H γ) for γ ∈ (0, 1].\\nKeywords: KdV equation, rough data, low-regularity integrator, stability, error estimates\\nAMS Subject Classification: 35Q53, 35B99, 65M12, 65M15.\\nContents\\n1 Introduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1\\n2 Notations and preliminary results. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 5\\n3 New tools for the construction of low-regularity integrators . . . . . . . . . 8\\n4 Construction of the low-regularity integrator . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 19\\n5 Reduction to a perturbed KdV equation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 24\\n6 Estimates for the local error Rn\\n2 [vn] . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 26\\n7 Estimates for the global remainder R(t) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 32\\n8 Stability estimates using low-high frequency decompositions . . . . . . . . . 39\\n9 Error estimates (Proof of Theorem 1.1) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 43\\n10 Numerical experiments . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 44\\n11 Conclusions and extended studies. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 46\\n1. Introduction\\nThe Korteweg–De Vries (KdV) equation is a nonlinear dispersive partial differential equa-\\ntion that describes many physical phenomena, including shallow water waves, ion acoustic\\n1\\n\\n2\\nwaves in plasmas, acoustic waves on crystal lattices, and so on. The development of compu-\\ntational methods for the KdV equation has attracted much attention.\\nIt is known that the KdV equation, either on a torus or on the whole space, is globally well-\\nposed in H s with s ≥ 0, i.e., there exists a unique solution in C([0, T]; H s) for any initial value\\nin H s; see [1,10,12,33]. However, classical time discretizations for the KdV equation, including\\nfinite difference methods, splitting methods, discontinuous Galerkin methods, and classical\\nexponential integrators, generally require much higher regularity for the numerical solutions\\nto converge with certain orders, i.e., these methods typically require u ∈ C([0, T]; H 3) and\\nu ∈ C([0, T]; H 6) to have first- and second-order convergence in L2, respectively. The error\\nestimates under these regularity conditions (or stronger conditions) for the above-mentioned\\nclassical time discretizations have been established, for example, in [7–9, 16, 17, 21, 25, 27, 32].\\nSuch regularity conditions are not mere technical conditions required in the error analysis.\\nWhen the solution of the KdV equation does not have the required regularity, its numerical\\napproximations by the classical time discretizations generally have reduced order of conver-\\ngence.\\nIn practice, the solutions of the KdV equation may be rough due to measurement or\\nrandomness of the data [2, 5]. To address the numerical approximation to nonsmooth so-\\nlutions, some low-regularity exponential integrators based on resonance analysis were re-\\ncently developed to relax the regularity requirement in solving nonlinear dispersive equations.\\nSuch low-regularity integrators based on resonance analysis were initially introduced by Hof-\\nmanov´ a & Schratz [6] and Ostermann & Schratz [18, 20] for solving the KdV equation and\\nthe nonlinear Schr¨ odinger (NLS) equation, respectively. In particular, for the KdV equa-\\ntion, the low-regularity integrator proposed in [6] can have first-order convergence in H 1 for\\nu ∈ C([0, T]; H 3). Wu & Zhao [30] showed that another method outlined in [6] can have\\nsecond-order convergence in H γ for u ∈ C([0, T]; H γ+4) with γ ≥ 0. In a more recent arti-\\ncle, Wu & Zhao [31] proposed two embedded low-regularity integrators for the KdV equation,\\nwhich have first-order convergence inH γ for u ∈ C([0, T]; H γ+1) with γ >1\\n2 and second-order\\nconvergence H γ for u ∈ C([0, T]; H γ+3) with γ ≥ 0, respectively. The minimal regularity re-\\nquirement for the convergence analysis of these unfiltered algorithms for the KdV equation is\\nu ∈ C([0, T]; H γ) for γ >3/2. This condition naturally arises in the energy type of stability\\nanalysis.\\nThe convergence of a fully discrete finite difference method was proved in [4] for u ∈\\nC([0, T]; H γ) with γ ≥ 3/4 under the CFL condition ∆ t ≤ ∆x3, where ∆ t and ∆ x denote\\nthe stepsize and mesh size in the temporal and spatial discretizations, respectively. The CFL\\ncondition in a finite difference method plays a similar role as the filters in a spectral method,\\ni.e., to improve the stability of the method under low-regularity conditions. In the case of\\nγ = 3/4, the method is proved convergent with order 1 /42. Since the convergence analysis\\nrelies on the smoothing effect on R, the proof cannot be extended to the torus T.\\nSimilarly, the development of low-regularity integrators for the NLS equations can be found\\nin [13, 14, 23, 29]. The minimal regularity requirement for the convergence analysis of the\\nunfiltered algorithms for the NLS equation is u ∈ C([0, T]; H γ) for γ > d/2, where d is the\\ndimension of space. This condition also arises in the energy stability analysis, which require\\nusing the Kato–Ponce inequality ∥f g∥H γ ≲ ∥f ∥H γ ∥g∥H γ with γ >d\\n2 . The question of whether\\n\\n3\\nany convergence rates can be achieved for rough solutionsu ∈ C([0, T]; H γ), with an arbitrary\\nsmall γ >0, remained open for a long time for both the KdV equation and the NLS equation.\\nThe convergence of numerical solutions for rough solutions in C([0, T]; H γ) with an ar-\\nbitrary small γ > 0 was addressed by Ostermann, Rousset & Schratz [19] and Rousset &\\nSchratz [24] for the NLS and KdV equations, respectively, by introducing and utilizing the\\ndiscrete Bourgain spaces. In particular, for the KdV equation, Rousset & Schratz [24] pro-\\nposed three filtered time discretizations for the KdV equation on the torus T (which cut\\nnumerical solutions to frequencies below τ − 1\\n3 , where τ denotes the step size of time dis-\\ncretization), including a filtered exponential integrator, a filtered Lie splitting method, and a\\nfiltered version of the resonance based scheme, and proved the convergence of order γ\\n3 for the\\nthree methods under the regularity condition u ∈ C([0, T]; H γ) with γ ∈ (0, 3]. The conver-\\ngence analysis in [24] is based on the combination of filters in the algorithms and the discrete\\nBourgain spaces in the analysis. Since the filters in these algorithms truncate the numerical\\nsolutions to frequencies below τ − 1\\n3 , and such frequency-truncated functions approximate the\\noriginal functions in H γ with an error bound of O(τ\\nγ\\n3 ), it follows that the convergence of such\\nfiltered algorithms is limited to order γ\\n3 for the KdV equation under the regularity condition\\nu ∈ C([0, T]; H γ).\\nThis article is concerned with the construction and analysis of new time discretizations for\\nthe KdV equation on a torus,\\n\\n\\n\\n∂tu(t, x) + ∂3\\nxu(t, x) = 1\\n2 ∂x(u(t, x)2), x ∈ T = [0, 2π] and t ∈ (0, T],\\nu(0, x) = u0(x), x ∈ T,\\n(1.1)\\nfor low-regularity solutions below H 1, i.e., the initial value u0 is in H γ with γ ∈ (0, 1] and\\ntherefore the solution u is in C([0, T]; H γ) with γ ∈ (0, 1]. One of the main difficulties in the\\nconstruction and analysis of low-regularity integrators for nonlinear dispersive equations is the\\napproximation of exponential functions with imaginary powers, say e isϕ, based on a certain\\ndecomposition of the phase function ϕ = ϕ1 + ϕ2. The approximation of such exponential\\nfunctions with imaginary powers were typically based on the following techniques:\\neisϕ = eisϕ1 + O(s|ϕ2|) or e isϕ = eisϕ1 + eisϕ2 − 1 + O(min{s|ϕ1|, s|ϕ2|}), (1.2)\\nsee [6, 13, 14, 23, 29, 31] and the references therein. The remainders in these types of approxi-\\nmations are still too large to obtain error estimates for rough solutions u ∈ C([0, T]; H γ) with\\nγ ∈ (0, 1]. In this article we introduce a new averaging approximation technique:\\nMτ\\n\\u0000\\neis(ϕ1+ϕ2)\\u0001\\n= Mτ\\n\\u0000\\neisϕ1\\n\\u0001\\nMτ\\n\\u0000\\neisϕ2\\n\\u0001\\n+ O\\n\\u0012\\nmin\\n\\u001a\\f\\f\\f\\f\\nϕ1\\nϕ2\\n\\f\\f\\f\\f ,\\n\\f\\f\\f\\f\\nϕ2\\nϕ1\\n\\f\\f\\f\\f , s|ϕ1|, s|ϕ2|\\n\\u001b\\u0013\\n, (1.3)\\nwhere Mτ (f ) denotes the average of a function f in the interval [0 , τ]; see Lemma 3.2. The\\nremainder in this approximation is smaller than the remainders in (1.2). In particular, the\\nadditional upper bounds |ϕ1/ϕ2| and |ϕ2/ϕ1| for the remainder are important for us to obtain\\nerror estimates in the rough case by using harmonic analysis techniques.\\nMoreover, it is known that the combination of filters and discrete Bourgain spaces in [24]\\nhas played an important role in establishing the stability of numerical approximations to\\nrough solutions. In this article, we develop new techniques which can be used to establish\\n\\n4\\nstability estimates under such low-regularity conditions when the energy techniques fail and\\nfilters are not used. More specifically, instead of using energy techniques locally in time, we\\ndefine a temporally continuous function V (t) which equals the numerical solution vn at the\\ndiscrete time levels tn, n = 0, 1, . . . , L, and satisfies an integral formulation of the continuous\\nKdV equation globally in time up to a perturbation term, i.e.,\\nV (t) = v0 + 1\\n2\\nZ t\\n0\\nes∂3\\nx∂x\\n\\u0010\\ne−s∂3\\nxV (s)\\n\\u00112\\nds + R(t) for t ∈ (0, T],\\nwhere the perturbation term R(t) can be defined piecewisely on each subinterval ( tn, tn+1]\\naccording to the definition of the time discretization on this subinterval. The specific form of\\nthe perturbation term for the low-regularity integrator constructed in this article is given in\\n(5.7). In the absence of the perturbation term R(t), the solution of the integral equation above\\ncoincides with the solution to the KdV equation. The continuous formulation of the numerical\\nscheme allows us to apply many harmonic analysis techniques at the continuous level, such as\\nlow- and high-frequency decomposition in estimating the stability of solutions with respect to\\nperturbation. These techniques can significantly weaken the regularity conditions compared\\nwith the energy approach of stability estimates used in numerical analysis.\\nIn addition, we establish some new harmonic analysis tools, including new frequency decom-\\nposition techniques (Lemma 3.3) and new trilinear estimates of the KdV operator (Propo-\\nsition 3.4), which can be used to construct and analyze low-regularity integrators without\\nusing filters (that cut numerical solutions to frequencies below τ − 1\\n3 ) and therefore improves\\nthe convergence order from γ\\n3 to γ (up to a logarithmic factor) under the regularity condition\\nu ∈ C([0, T]; H γ) for γ ∈ (0, 1].\\nFor the convenience of readers, we present the numerical scheme and the main theoretical\\nresult below. Let tn = nτ , n = 0, 1, . . . , L= T /τ, be a partition of the time interval [0 , T]\\nwith stepsize τ = T /L. The low-regularity integrator constructed in this article for the KdV\\nequation (11.1) reads: For given un ∈ H γ, find un+1 ∈ H γ by\\nun+1 =e−τ ∂3\\nxun + F [un] + H[un] for n = 0, 1 . . . , L− 1, (1.4)\\nwhere\\nF [un] = 1\\n6 P\\n\\u0002\\u0000\\ne−τ ∂3\\nx∂−1\\nx un\\u00012\\u0003\\n− 1\\n6 e−τ ∂3\\nxP\\n\\u0002\\u0000\\n∂−1\\nx un\\u00012\\u0003\\n,\\nH[un] = 1\\n3 P\\nh\\u0000\\ne−τ ∂3\\nx∂−1\\nx un\\u0001\\n∂−1\\nx F [un]\\ni\\n+ τ\\n9 e−τ ∂3\\nx(∂−1\\nx un) P0[(un)2]\\n− 1\\n54 e(s−τ )∂3\\nx∂−1\\nx\\n\\u0002\\n(e−s∂3\\nx∂−1\\nx un)3\\u0003\\f\\f\\f\\f\\ns=τ\\ns=0\\n− 1\\n27τ e(s−τ )∂3\\nx∂−2\\nx\\nh\\n(e−(s−τ )∂3\\nx∂−2\\nx F [un]) (e−s∂3\\nx∂−1\\nx un)\\ni\\f\\f\\f\\ns=τ\\ns=0\\n,\\nin which P0 and P are projection operators define\", \"next_offset\": 12000, \"has_more\": true, \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"external-sources/access-90b7137698f84c96813a9ed60ce2d342.json\", \"sha256\": \"4fd4ae656067669f99ee0e18cf0d54d6aa11031c7e5963641d237664e6bfd535\"}, {\"path\": \"external-sources/access-90b7137698f84c96813a9ed60ce2d342.pdf\", \"sha256\": \"e71452d13d7d54b902c6d79a08bbcd68edab795f0a59366fff14399b98192f9b\"}, {\"path\": \"external-sources/access-90b7137698f84c96813a9ed60ce2d342.txt\", \"sha256\": \"0f444ea8653c6f0f1f46b8446362060aa1ffafb328148f88bab72154fc62e22d\"}], \"access_record\": {\"path\": \"external-sources/access-90b7137698f84c96813a9ed60ce2d342.json\", \"sha256\": \"4fd4ae656067669f99ee0e18cf0d54d6aa11031c7e5963641d237664e6bfd535\"}, \"primary_evidence_candidates\": [{\"path\": \"external-sources/access-90b7137698f84c96813a9ed60ce2d342.pdf\", \"sha256\": \"e71452d13d7d54b902c6d79a08bbcd68edab795f0a59366fff14399b98192f9b\"}, {\"path\": \"external-sources/access-90b7137698f84c96813a9ed60ce2d342.txt\", \"sha256\": \"0f444ea8653c6f0f1f46b8446362060aa1ffafb328148f88bab72154fc62e22d\"}], \"evidence_scope\": \"Readability and transport only. Identify the original complete source before designating primary evidence; search/abstract pages are not theorem verification.\"}"}
{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"version\": \"yui-source-access-1\", \"origin\": \"controller_https\", \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0910.1389v2\", \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-10-31T21:27:21.401117+00:00\", \"status\": \"READABLE\", \"final_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0910.1389v2\", \"http_status\": 200, \"document\": {\"path\": \"/Users/syyui/projects/Yui-results/benchmark/results/20261031T212422Z-533b379994/nl0/0001-0001/stage/case/external-sources/access-056aeaa38c034c57ad77098d4fbf334d.pdf\", \"sha256\": \"b6958c0b155bb8463f85900c4beaf75b1cf399382101a720cc39edf82cfac52e\"}, \"document_kind\": \"PDF\", \"links\": [], \"doi_candidates\": [], \"text\": {\"path\": \"/Users/syyui/projects/Yui-results/benchmark/results/20261031T212422Z-533b379994/nl0/0001-0001/stage/case/external-sources/access-056aeaa38c034c57ad77098d4fbf334d.txt\", \"sha256\": \"3a37d646c752a691c70b1b58bcd3525e413905f83cb9f3e988c1174871108683\"}, \"record\": {\"path\": \"/Users/syyui/projects/Yui-results/benchmark/results/20261031T212422Z-533b379994/nl0/0001-0001/stage/case/external-sources/access-056aeaa38c034c57ad77098d4fbf334d.json\", \"sha256\": \"d0c68a73bf3a61fbec9636214f6f868e0d6b0cae142c8f4181f280d0b31f49e4\"}, \"content\": \"arXiv:0910.1389v2  [math.AP]  23 Oct 2010\\nON THE REGULARIZATION MECHANISM FOR THE PERIODIC\\nKORTEWEG–DE VRIES EQUATION\\nAnatoli V. Babin 1, Alexei A. Ilyin 2 and Edriss S. Titi 3\\nThis paper is dedicated to the memory of Professor Basil Nicolaenko\\nAbstract. In this paper we develop and use successive averaging method s for explaining the regular-\\nization mechanism in the the periodic Korteweg–de Vries (Kd V) equation in the homogeneous Sobolev\\nspaces ˙H s, for s ≥ 0. Speciﬁcally, we prove the global existence, uniqueness, and Lipschitz continuous\\ndependence on the initial data of the solutions of the period ic KdV. For the case where the initial data\\nis in L2 we also show the Lipschitz continuous dependence of these so lutions with respect to the initial\\ndata as maps from ˙H s to ˙H s, for s ∈ (−1, 0].\\nKeywords: Korteweg–de Vries equation, dispersive equations, averaging met hod, non-\\nlinear resonance.\\nMSC: 35B34,35Q53.\\n1. Introduction\\nThis paper is motivated by works on global regularity of solutions of 3 D problems in\\nhydrodynamics (Navier-Stokes or Boussinesq system with periodic boundary conditions)\\nin the presence of fast rotation or strong stratiﬁcation, see, e.g ., [1], [2], [3], [4], [11],\\n[12], [18], [16], [20], [26], and references therein. The results are base d on the presence of\\nhigh-frequency waves that lead to destructive interference and eﬀectively weaken the non-\\nlinearity through time averaging allowing to prove global regularity of solutions for these\\nproblems, at the limit of inﬁnite rotation. The above mentioned hydro dynamical problems\\nare rather complicated (Boussinesq system, for example, involves ﬁve unknown functions of\\nthree spatial variables and time, the equations are coupled throug h a quadratic nonlinear-\\nity). Therefore, the purpose of this paper is to apply this averagin g approach to a slightly\\nsimpler problem to make these ideas more transparent. Here we con sider the Korteweg–de\\nVries equation with one spatial variable subjected to periodic bound ary condition. The\\naveraging eﬀects are strong because on high Fourier modes the line ar term generates high-\\nfrequency oscillations which weakens the nonlinearity and makes it mild er. In this paper\\nwe show connections between the smoothness properti…8129 tokens truncated…on, relevant canonical content and\\n92:    source evidence. Review each D and selected E/M; the overview is data, not\\n93:    an assessment. Do not infer absence from a partial page.\\n94: 2. Complete global source/graph review before dispatching local R reviews.\\n95:    Up to the explicitly confirmed N independent R reviewers work concurrently.\\n96:    Each receives only its own packet and mathematical-NL0 rules. Review full arguments,\\n97:    actual applications and definitions; input N statements are conditional.\\n98: 3. Prefer one complete artifact with an explicit coverage map. Use\\n99:    record_review_item and read_review_progress only for useful checkpoints.\\n100:    record_background_note is optional; a complete artifact may include its\\n101:    actual notes directly. Batch reading several declared fragments is available.\\n102: 4. Each local report has inference, packet_sha256, verdict, notes, checks,\\n103:    findings and optional background_notes. The checks map contains exactly\\n104:    statement_closure, dependency_closure, argument, applicability, packet_replay.\\n105:    Each row has verdict, notes and resolving packet locations. E applications\\n106:    additionally retain actual binding, per-hypothesis, conclusion and bridge\\n107:    assessments; this is mathematical applicability, not a Lean API inventory.\\n108: 5. Global reports have target_review, definition_reviews, source_reviews,\\n109:    structure_review and findings. Each E adds citation_checks for\\n110:    literature_identity, result_location, statement_fidelity,\\n111:    context_completeness, version_and_eligibility. Inaccessible primary text\\n112:    leaves blocked checks UNVERIFIED while remaining assessment continues.\\n113: 6. A blocking finding needs its actual location, explanation and a short exact\\n114:    quotation from that location. Preserve the defect and untouched coverage.\\n115:    Return located mathematical repairs to the author; review changed scopes\\n116:    independently. Do not resample a mathematical rejection or silently erase it.\\n117: 7. The collector checks complete coverage, consistency, exact bytes and actual\\n118:    independent execution before producing the audit-format-4 receipt.\\n119:    Old detailed-NL0 format-3 receipts are not silently upgraded. Inherited format-4 judgments retain\\n120:    their original source/rule hashes, exposure and costs.\\n121: \\n122: ## Sources, scopes and final handoff\\n123: \\n124: Use [external theorem review](external-theorems.md) and\\n125: [conditional sources](conditional-sources.md) for source identity, exact\\n126: statements and warnings. Reuse adequate original text; several theorem uses\\n127: may share one staged readback. Each application still discharges its own\\n128: premises. No successful download or guessed Mathlib availability is approval.\\n129: \\n130: Use [logical rules](logical-rules.md) for local scopes and\\n131: [background notes](background-notes.md) for the mathematical supplement.\\n132: The ordinary complete result remains relative to explicit E assumptions.\\n133: An inaccessible E original permits only a source-qualified result. Translation\\n134: retains this exact audit and supplement until reviewed mathematical changes\\n135: invalidate affected scopes. Typed interfaces, native checking, standalone\\n136: assembly and independent semantic Audit B are separate later gates.\\n137: \\n138: ## Paywalled or inaccessible primary text\\n139: \\n140: Follow [conditional sources](conditional-sources.md). Preserve the complete\\n141: secondary statement and actual access evidence; unavailable source checks\\n142: stay UNVERIFIED. Continue other work only with an explicitly qualified source\\n143: assumption, and report warn.json/warn.md. No unqualified full PASS is granted.\"}"}
{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/nl0-format.md\", \"total_lines\": 426, \"start\": 56, \"end\": 95, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 0, \"requested_end\": 95, \"has_more\": true, \"next_start\": 96, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": 345, \"text\": \"56: - `format_version: 2`, `case_id`, `full_target`, `conclusion` (nonempty strings);\\n57: - `parameters`: records `{symbol, type}`; parameter symbols reserve global names.\\n58:   Give ambient data types/structures only. Original conditions, ranges and\\n59:   equations belong in H, not in parameter refinements that would be visible\\n60:   to every R;\\n61: - `hypotheses`: records `{id: Hddd, statement, origin}` extracted before authoring;\\n62: - `definitions`: records `{id: Dddd, symbol, definition, type, depends_on,\\n63:   requires, binders, objects}`. Each symbol has exactly one global meaning. `depends_on` lists\\n64:   earlier/resolvable D IDs and is acyclic. `requires` lists proved H/N IDs that\\n65:   must already be available whenever the definition is used;\\n66: - `logic`: `{classical: boolean, rules: [names], calculations: [fixed IDs],\\n67:   primitives: [{symbol, type, meaning}]}`. In the clean author tool, omit\\n68:   `calculations` and `primitives` to bind the controller's fixed language profile.\\n69:   Optional calculation IDs are `equality`, `definitions`, `finite_algebra`,\\n70:   `finite_order`, `finite_sums`. Optional primitive selections may be\\n71:   `{symbol: ...}`; the controller validates selections and binds the complete\\n72:   core syntax with exact type/meaning (numerals, number systems and empty-set/top\\n73:   notation). This is not a subject vocabulary or a list of allowed theorems. A new\\n74:   Audit A requires the bound metadata. Author-defined permission descriptions\\n75:   and conflicting primitive meanings are rejected, not silently accepted;\\n76: - `pins`: `{lean_version, mathlib_commit, mathlib_root}`; the root is a local\\n77:   locator, the version/commit are scientific identifiers;\\n78: - `inputs`: evidence records `{path, sha256}` for the original problem/proof.\\n79: \\n80: The exact machine identifiers permitted in `logic.rules` and inference `rules`\\n81: are `direct`, `modus_ponens`, `implication_introduction`, `negation_introduction`,\\n82: `contradiction_classical`, `case_elimination`, `forall_introduction`,\\n83: `exists_elimination`, and `natural_induction`. Describe algebra/analysis in the\\n84: actual proof text; it is not a new trusted logical rule. These identifiers\\n85: describe proof packaging, not a substitute for establishing the implication.\\n86: \\n87: The `elementary-language-4` profile in `nl0/scripts/foundations.py` permits\\n88: displayed finite algebra/order calculations and ordinary logic. Its core syntax\\n89: contains no domain-specific vocabulary. Objects and notation from any subject\\n90: are parameters, D definitions or scoped locals, with explicit meaning, type and\\n91: dependencies; they cannot be hidden in primitives. Recognizable notation grants\\n92: no identity, existence or regularity. No global dictionary extension is needed\\n93: to represent a new subject.\\n94: For every substantive fact still register an E/M input or give its local proof.\\n95: \"}"}
{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/templates/authoring.md\", \"total_lines\": 179, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 179, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 66, \"requested_end\": 200, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # NL0 construction and tool template\\n2: \\n3: Use [Audit A's shared criteria](../references/audit-a.md#shared-mathematical-acceptance-requirements)\\n4: as the mathematical authority. This template explains data/tool construction;\\n5: it is not a parallel acceptance policy. Read the [schema](../references/nl0-format.md),\\n6: [construction guide](../references/nl0-authoring.md), and the complete neutral\\n7: [submission](submission-example.md), [source application](source-application-example.md)\\n8: and [quantified implication](quantified-implication-example.md) examples.\\n9: These examples show format and derivations, not certified benchmark answers.\\n10: \\n11: The complete [guarded-definition example](guarded-definition-example.md) shows\\n12: how to organize a target that includes an object's existence and a property\\n13: of that object. For a real a let Q(r) mean r=a. R001 first proves N001, unique\\n14: existence of a Q witness, without using w. D002 then defines w as that unique\\n15: witness with requires=[N001]. R002 takes N001 as a direct input and proves the\\n16: full target: unique existence and w=a. Listing D002 in the global spec does\\n17: not let R001 use it before its guard. Do not hide an unproved guard in a\\n18: restricted domain when the frozen target requires a statement on the full\\n19: original domain. This pattern adds no new assumption or mathematical source.\\n20: \\n21: ## Construct one coherent candidate\\n22: \\n23: 1. For `target` and `definitions`, extract the original H, ambient parameters\\n24:    and global typed definitions. Preserve the original source locations. Do\\n25:    not change a parameter's mathematical kind while fixing its metadata.\\n26: 2. For `statement_closure`, design N/R interfaces from what their\\n27:    consumers actually need. Put the complete formulas, quantified conditions\\n28:    and space identifications into the exported statement, not only its body.\\n29: 3. For `dependency_closure` and `argument`, construct\\n30:    each proof unit alongside its premises. List exact source inputs or display\\n31:    a prior local derivation for every substantive fact beyond the fixed\\n32:    calculations. Split when local context changes; avoid a giant S whose\\n33:    changing objects must later be repaired one at a time.\\n34: 4. For `sources` and `applicability`, inspect each selected native/primary\\n35:    source, preserve its complete contract and ground each instantiated premise.\\n36:    A signature lookup or a sentence claiming a hypothesis is true is not proof\\n37:    of that hypothesis. Reuse an adequate proved source rather than re-proving\\n38:    it; a project-specific bridge still requires its displayed derivation.\\n39: 5. For object declarations and local scopes under dependency_closure, introduce objects and conditional premises\\n40:    at their actual first use. Keep witness bases and local F dependencies\\n41:    correct. Preserve exact coefficients and ranges when copying calculations.\\n42: 6. Run check_draft, correct its actual located records, inspect every own\\n43:    conditional packet, and report readiness below. The independent auditor\\n44:    applies the same criteria after definitive submission.\\n45: \\n46: ## Canonical data shapes\\n47: \\n48: Submit {spec,graph} using the schema, or preserve an own draft with\\n49: {base_candidate:N,patches:[...]}. The controller binds identity, input/pin\\n50: hashes, fixed language and exact native metadata; it does not rewrite proof\\n51: content. Use read_draft with a narrow pointer after an edit rejection.\\n52: Unique existing @ID/@symbol selectors work for own-stage reads/patches;\\n53: inserting array elements still uses numeric indices or /-. Audit evidence\\n54: uses standard JSON pointers instead of this selector extension.\\n55: \\n56: For multiline changes prefer read_draft(candidate=N,pointer=\\\"/graph/inferences/@R001/argument/@S001/text\\\",\\n57: start_line=1,end_line=20). It returns the actual numbered lines, total_lines\\n58: and complete text_sha256. Use an edit with exactly\\n59: {op:\\\"replace_lines\\\",path:\\\"/graph/inferences/@R001/argument/@S001/text\\\",start_line:4,end_line:5,\\n60: text:\\\"replacement including its intended line endings\\\",text_sha256:\\\"the returned hash\\\"}.\\n61: Ranges are positive and inclusive. Unselected text and line endings are\\n62: preserved; stale hashes are rejected. Include a line terminator when a\\n63: nonempty replacement precedes retained lines. Empty text deletes the range.\\n64: All ordinary validation and later Audit A still apply.\\n65: For reads only, an end beyond the string returns its remaining existing lines\\n66: and the actual range; the start must exist and at most 250 lines may be\\n67: requested. Line edits still reject any out-of-range end and never clamp it.\\n68: \\n69: Each D has type, full definition, binders, used objects, D dependencies and\\n70: proved guards. Each H/E/M/N/P has full statement, definitions, binders, objects\\n71: and appropriate source/origin fields. Each R has direct inputs, one output,\\n72: definitions, rules, locals, argument and real source applications. Each S has\\n73: actual text, uses, earlier uses_local, objects, complete produces F statements,\\n74: and assumption introductions/discharges. Each source application has input,\\n75: at:Sddd, bindings and actual hypothesis_checks. See the linked schema/examples\\n76: for exact names. source_uses can serialize one already selected M/E into the\\n77: R.inputs, S.uses and application together; supply the actual mathematical\\n78: bindings and grounds yourself.\\n79: \\n80: When a declared frozen task already supplies a complete adequate definition,\\n81: D.definition may be {from_task:\\\"input ID\\\",start_line:N,end_line:M}, just like\\n82: S.text. The same form is available for spec.hypotheses[i].statement when the\\n83: selected passage states a complete original GIVEN. Keep its origin; never\\n84: promote a desired conclusion or proof claim to H. An H referring to the\\n85: target/problem for its missing content is not standalone.\\n86: The controller copies those exact lines to an ordinary canonical\\n87: string and records their hashes; it does not select or correct the definition.\\n88: Still declare its objects, binders, dependencies and guards. Normalize any\\n89: needed notation explicitly and preserve the supplied meaning. Copying never\\n90: grants a property or a mathematical PASS.\\n91: \\n92: A local declaration has symbol, type, scope, start/end, kind, declaration,\\n93: from_inputs, from_local and objects. from_local holds earlier F proof IDs;\\n94: ordinary object dependencies belong in objects. A fact proved later in this\\n95: same paragraph is not yet a premise for choosing its witness. State it in an\\n96: earlier S. In sum_{k=0}^m a(k), k is expression-bound; the free objects are a,m.\\n97: A k used outside the sum needs its own declaration. Statement binders are\\n98: recorded with that statement; a variable spanning S paragraphs uses locals.\\n99: Changing a ledger entry does not rename an existing formula.\\n100: \\n101: ## Actual own-packet inspection and release\\n102: \\n103: After structural validation call read_author_packet(candidate,inference) for\\n104: every R. The returned view contains the real direct input/output statements,\\n105: definitions, logic and own proof, plus the shared contract binding. Read the\\n106: output without remembering its producer body. For each factual invocation,\\n107: identify its actual input or previous derivation. Complete closing notes use\\n108: these existing evidence groups; their criterion IDs are supplied by the\\n109: controller from Audit A. Cite actual H/M/E/N/F/R/S or candidate locations in\\n110: those notes; generic assurances are insufficient.\\n111: \\n112: record_author_checks has this exact tool form (one row per actual N/P and R):\\n113: \\n114: ```json\\n115: {\\n116:   \\\"candidate\\\": 7,\\n117:   \\\"readiness\\\": \\\"no_known_issue\\\",\\n118:   \\\"open_issues\\\": [],\\n119:   \\\"resolutions\\\": [],\\n120:   \\\"original_context_note\\\": \\\"Located comparison of original target/H, definitions, selected source contracts and graph closure.\\\",\\n121:   \\\"output_notes\\\": [{\\\"id\\\": \\\"P\\\", \\\"note\\\": \\\"Located check of the full output and the derivation of every clause.\\\"}],\\n122:   \\\"inference_notes\\\": [{\\n123:     \\\"id\\\": \\\"R001\\\",\\n124:     \\\"dependency_and_local_assumption_check\\\": \\\"Actual factual grounds, calculation, compatible inputs and discharged assumptions, with locations.\\\",\\n125:     \\\"source_application_check\\\": \\\"Actual substitutions and grounds for every source premise, or why no source is used.\\\",\\n126:     \\\"declaration_check\\\": \\\"Actual object introductions, types, witness bases and scopes, with locations.\\\"\\n127:   }]\\n128: }\\n129: ```\\n130: \\n131: These are author claims, not PASS verdicts. If an obligation is unfinished,\\n132: set readiness=unresolved and list an issue such as\\n133: {id:\\\"I001\\\",criterion:\\\"dependency_closure\\\",location:\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\\\",note:\\\"The exact missing input/derivation.\\\"}.\\n134: Use not_checked for unperformed inspection; incomplete notes can be saved,\\n135: but cannot release the artifact. Known issues persist across candidate edits.\\n136: Once corrected, provide resolutions:[{id:\\\"I001\\\",location:\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\\\",note:\\\"The actual correction or justified withdrawal and its grounds.\\\"}].\\n137: Both issue and resolution locations must exist in the current candidate.\\n138: The context note checks the actual definition types and their prior guards;\\n139: inference notes check the exact source conclusion and any needed change of\\n140: domain or representation, rather than listing source names alone.\\n141: An empty new list cannot silently erase an old issue. The program checks\\n142: coverage, explicit state, locations and content/authority binding; mathematical\\n143: soundness still needs independent Audit A.\\n144: \\n145: An actual own-packet read remains credited after an unrelated candidate edit\\n146: only when its complete displayed mathematical view and acceptance authority\\n147: have identical canonical contents. The recorded hash covers parameters, logic, selected\\n148: definitions, actual direct inputs/source contracts, output and full argument.\\n149: Changed views require new actual reads. Current whole-candidate release notes\\n150: are still invalidated by every edit, and known issues still need resolutions.\\n151: These are same-author read receipts, never inherited mathematical verdicts.\\n152: \\n153: Only author_release_ready allows complete-artifact submission. Otherwise\\n154: continue the actual local proof/source work in this same stage. At a real\\n155: budget limit preserve the draft as unfinished. Normal completion difficulties\\n156: are not a substantive mathematical-gap report. Source/graph edits invalidate\\n157: bound closing notes. When ready, resubmit the exact candidate. Fresh Audit A\\n158: receives none of these notes or historical feedback.\\n159: \\n160: ## Library access\\n161: \\n162: find_library_sources offers a small verified pinned metadata index, not a\\n163: whitelist. An exact canonical name selects that declaration; ordinary terms\\n164: return ranked candidates with actual matched terms. Overlap does not establish\\n165: applicability. probe_library resolves genuine aliases and the actual module;\\n166: its optional module argument is only a locator hint. Read the pinned source\\n167: and compare its whole native signature with its NL restatement and actual\\n168: application. Predicates/data definitions are not source theorems. Same-stage\\n169: M-label renumbering can reuse exact metadata through a disclosed mapping;\\n170: it does not rewrite any mathematical statement or applicability argument.\\n171: \\n172: ## Background granularity\\n173: \\n174: Follow Audit A's shared verified-background admission rule. Register the main\\n175: tools; write ordinary rigorous mathematics rather than anticipating every\\n176: small Lean lemma. Correct elementary compression is allowed. Audit A can\\n177: verify an omitted routine library fact and attach a note without an author\\n178: rewrite. Missing original H, source uncertainty and substantive proof gaps\\n179: remain blocking. These audit notes later accompany each Lean task.\"}"}

