s("S001","H001-H003 give T>0, 0<gamma<=1 and R>0. Fix a0 and temporarily assume L1: a0 is real, mean-zero, in H^gamma and ||a0||_(H^gamma)<=R. Set U0(x)=-a0(-x), so hat U0(k)=-hat a0(-k). It is real and mean-zero and preserves all Fourier Sobolev norms. In particular ||U0||_(dot H^gamma)<=R and ||U0||_2<=R. Apply E001 with s=gamma,Q=T,U_0=U0. Its hypotheses hold by these norm facts and H001-H002. It supplies a unique continuous source flow U, a uniform homogeneous bound depending only on T,gamma,R, and smooth-approximation construction. Record its existence as F001.",["H001","H002","H003","E001"],[],["T","gamma","R","a0","U0","Fourier","H","Hdot"],[f("F001","There exists the unique source solution with initial datum U0 on [0,T], continuous in dot H^gamma, bounded there by a constant depending only on T,gamma,R, constructed by smooth approximation.",["U0","T","gamma","R","Hdot"],["L1"])],["L1"]),
s("S002","Choose U from F001 and set u(t,x)=-U(t,-x). For smooth solutions u_t=-U_t, u_x=U_x and u_xxx=U_xxx at (t,-x), so u_t+u_xxx=-UU_x=u*u_x=(1/2)partial_x(u^2). The same equality follows distributionally by the reflection change of variables for the limiting solution. Fourier coefficients satisfy hat u(t)(k)=-hat U(t)(-k), hence all Sobolev norms, zero mean and reality are preserved. The comparison ||u||_(H^gamma)<=2^(gamma/2)||U||_(dot H^gamma) gives a bound A(T,gamma,R). Finite Fourier norm sums pass to the limit under smooth approximation, and their supremum gives the full bound; continuity gives it for every time. Uniqueness follows by applying the inverse reflection to two solutions, using the source coefficient formulation, equivalent to the distributional equation for continuous L^2 paths by testing each Fourier mode. Set v=S_(-t)u. Unit-modulus multipliers preserve its H^gamma norm. Differentiation for smooth data gives v'=(1/2)B_t(v,v).",[],["F001"],["U","u","v","T","gamma","R","S","H","Hdot","Fourier","KdV","B"],[f("F002","Under L1 the exact u exists uniquely, sup_[0,T]||u||_(H^gamma)<=A(T,gamma,R), v=S_(-t)u has the same bound, and for smooth data v'=(1/2)B_t(v,v).",["u","v","T","gamma","R","S","H","B"],["L1"])]),
s("S003","For the smooth-data clause, temporarily assume L3: a0 is smooth. Apply E001 at every positive integer regularity s, with this same U0. Its finite higher norms and uniqueness make all these solutions coincide with U. Thus u is spatially smooth, and the equation supplies its time smoothness. Discharge L3 to obtain that conditional clause, and discharge L1 to obtain the stated assertion for every a0 in the ball. For the pairwise assertion fix arbitrary a0,b0 and temporarily assume L2: they are real mean-zero L^2 data whose norms sum to at most 2*R. Define W0=-b0(-x). Apply E002 with Q=T,theta=0,B0=2*R to U0,W0. Their zero mean, reality and norm sum are unchanged by reflection; theta=0 lies in (-1,0]. Its source common untwisting multiplier is an L^2 isometry, so E002 and reflection give sup||u_a0-u_b0||_2<=K(T,2*R)||a0-b0||_2. This includes the original H^gamma ball since each norm is <=R. Discharge L2. The constructions and bounds hold for every chosen datum; M=1+sup||v||_(H^gamma)<=1+A. This proves N001.",["E001","E002"],["F002"],["T","gamma","R","a0","b0","U0","W0","U","u","v","H","S"],[],["L3","L2"],["L3","L1","L2"])
],[
{id:"A001",input:"E001",at:"S001",bindings:"Q=T, s=gamma, source initial datum U0(x)=-a0(-x).",hypothesis_checks:["L1 gives real mean-zero a0; reflection preserves both properties on the same length-2*pi torus.","H001-H002 give Q>0 and s>0; Fourier norm comparison bounds dot H^gamma and L^2 norms by R under L1."]},
{id:"A002",input:"E001",at:"S003",bindings:"Q=T, source U0 as above, s ranges over the positive integers for the conditional smooth-data clause.",hypothesis_checks:["L1 and the reflection give the same real mean-zero periodic datum.","H001 gives T>0; under L3 all higher homogeneous norms are finite, so each source regularity condition is satisfied."]},
{id:"A003",input:"E002",at:"S003",bindings:"Q=T, theta=0, B0=2*R, source data U0=-a0(-x), W0=-b0(-x).",hypothesis_checks:["H001 gives Q>0 and 0 is in (-1,0].","L2 is the pairwise norm-sum antecedent; reflection preserves norms, reality and mean zero."]}
]);

prop("N002","For every tau>0 and alpha,beta in R, eta_tau(alpha,beta)=0 if alpha*beta=0. Otherwise |eta_tau(alpha,beta)|<=2*min(|alpha/beta|,|beta/alpha|) and |eta_tau(alpha,beta)|<=tau*min(|alpha|,|beta|).",[b("tau","positive real"),b("alpha","real"),b("beta","real")],["tau","alpha","beta","eta","Mavg"],ids("eta","Mavg"),"problem.md:145-161, covariance proof.");
r("R002",[],"N002",ids("eta","Mavg"),[
local("tau","positive real","arbitrary","Fix any tau>0.","S001","S002"),
local("alpha","real","arbitrary","Fix alpha in R.","S001","S002"),
local("beta","real","arbitrary","Fix beta in R.","S001","S002"),
local("h","smooth complex function on [0,tau]","definition","h(s)=exp(-i*alpha*s)-Mavg_tau(exp(-i*alpha*.)).","S001","S002",["tau","alpha","Mavg"])
],[
s("S001","Fix tau>0, alpha,beta real. If either phase vanishes its exponential is 1 and the covariance is zero. In the remaining case assume L1: alpha*beta!=0. Define h(s)=exp(-i*alpha*s)-Mavg_tau(exp(-i*alpha*.)). Then eta=tau^(-1)integral_0^tau h(s)exp(-i*beta*s)ds. The elementary integral estimate |exp(-i*alpha*s)-exp(-i*alpha*r)|<=|alpha||s-r| gives |h(s)|<=tau^(-1)integral_0^tau |alpha||s-r|dr<=tau|alpha|. Consequently |eta|<=tau|alpha|. At s=0,tau the sharper integrals are each tau|alpha|/2, so |h(0)|+|h(tau)|<=tau|alpha|; also integral_0^tau|h'(s)|ds=tau|alpha|.",[],[],["tau","alpha","beta","h","eta","Mavg"],[f("F001","Under L1, |eta_tau(alpha,beta)|<=tau|alpha| and |h(0)|+|h(tau)|+integral_0^tau|h'|<=2*tau|alpha|.",["tau","alpha","beta","h","eta"],["L1"])],["L1"]),
s("S002","Integration by parts against exp(-i*beta*s), which has nonzero derivative factor -i*beta under L1, gives |eta|<= (|h(0)|+|h(tau)|+integral|h'|)/(tau|beta|)<=2|alpha/beta| by F001. The defining covariance is symmetric in alpha,beta; repeating both estimates with the phases interchanged gives |eta|<=tau|beta| and |eta|<=2|beta/alpha|. Take their minima, discharge L1, and include the zero-phase case already calculated. Universal introduction proves N002.",[],["F001"],["tau","alpha","beta","h","eta"],[],[],["L1"])
]);

prop("N003","For nonzero integers a,b,c,d with a+b+c+d=0, let N1>=N2>=N3>=N4 be the rearranged magnitudes and A0=|a*b|,B0=|c*d|. Then N1<=3*N2, (1/|d|)min(A0/B0,B0/A0)<=3/N3 and |a+b|/max(A0,B0)<=6/N3. Moreover for any nonnegative x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4 in ell^2(Z\\{0}), sum_(a+b+c+d=0,abcd!=0) product_(j=1)^4 x_j(k_j)/N3(a,b,c,d)<=C*product_j||x_j||_(ell^2), where (k_1,k_2,k_3,k_4)=(a,b,c,d), and C is absolute. The same bound holds on any subset of these interactions, and the infinite positive sum is the supremum of its finite restrictions.",
["a","b","c","d"].map(z=>b(z,"nonzero integer")).concat([b("N1","largest magnitude"),b("N2","second largest magnitude"),b("N3","third largest magnitude"),b("N4","smallest magnitude"),b("A0","|a*b|"),b("B0","|c*d|"),...["x_1","x_2","x_3","x_4"].map(z=>b(z,"nonnegative ell^2 sequence on nonzero integers"))]),
