s("S001","Fix sigma,tau,t0 and polynomial inputs. In each nonzero term write k=a+b+c, p=a+b, d=-k, A0=|ab|,B0=|cd|, and let N3 be the third largest of |a|,|b|,|c|,|d|. Terms with a zero input frequency, zero k, or zero phase vanish by definition and N002. On the remaining terms alpha=3abp,beta=3kcp. The ratio bound of N002 and the first majorant of N003 give |eta|/|k|<=6/N3. The small-phase bound gives |eta|/(tau|abck|)<=3|p|min(A0,B0)/(A0*B0)=3|p|/max(A0,B0)<=18/N3 by N003. Their geometric interpolation with weights 1-sigma,sigma yields |eta|/(|k||abc|^sigma)<=6^(1-sigma)18^sigma*tau^sigma/N3. This includes sigma=0 and sigma=1 as the separate endpoint bounds.",["N002","N003"],[],["sigma","tau","t0","f_1","f_2","f_3","Rem","eta"],[f("F001","The interaction multiplier in N004 is bounded by 6^(1-sigma)18^sigma*tau^sigma/N3, including both endpoints and all zero terms.",["sigma","tau","eta"])]),
s("S002","Dualize the Fourier coefficients of Rem against a finitely supported unit ell^2 sequence. After absolute values, use F001 and N003 with x_1(a)=|a|^sigma|hat f_1(a)| and likewise for slots 2 and 3; take x_4(d) to be the modulus of the dual coefficient at -d. Their norms are bounded by the three H^sigma norms and the unit dual norm. The factor exp(-i*t0*Phi) has modulus one, and the prefactor in Rem is tau/18. Thus ||Rem||_2<=C_sigma*tau^(1+sigma)product_j||f_j||_(H^sigma). A multiplier of modulus <=1 only decreases the absolute majorant, so the same proof covers every stated restriction.",["N003"],["F001"],["sigma","tau","t0","f_1","f_2","f_3","Rem","Fourier","H"],[f("F002","For polynomial inputs, the stated trilinear norm bound, uniformly in support and with any fixed extra multiplier of modulus <=1, holds.",["sigma","tau","t0","f_1","f_2","f_3","Rem","H"])]),
s("S003","Approximate each mean-zero H^sigma input by its finite Fourier projections. The trilinear bound F002 and the three-term telescoping difference bound show that the outputs are Cauchy in the complete L^2 space, independently of the chosen approximations. This defines a unique bounded trilinear extension and retains the inequality. For smooth inputs it coincides with the original absolutely convergent Fourier expression. At sigma=0 this supplies in particular an L^2-by-L^2-by-L^2 bound C*tau.",[],["F002"],["sigma","tau","t0","f_1","f_2","f_3","Rem","H"],[f("F003","For every exponent in [0,1], the bounded trilinear extension and inequality asserted in N004 hold; at exponent zero its bound is C*tau times the product of L^2 norms.",["tau","t0","Rem","H"])]),
s("S004","For the final clause fix f,w,M0 and temporarily assume L1: 0<sigma<=1,0<tau<=1,w in mean-zero H^sigma with ||w||_(H^sigma)<=M0, f in mean-zero L^2, and delta=||f-w||_2<=1. Apply F003 with exponent sigma to (w,w,w). Expand Rem(f,f,f)-Rem(w,w,w) as Rem(f-w,f,f)+Rem(w,f-w,f)+Rem(w,w,f-w) and apply F003 with exponent zero to each term. The difference is bounded by C*tau*delta*(||f||_2+||w||_2)^2. Since ||f||_2<=M0+1, this yields C_(sigma,M0)*tau*(tau^sigma+delta). Discharge L1 and quantify all data. This proves N004.",[],["F003"],["sigma","tau","t0","f","w","M0","delta","Rem","H"],[],["L1"],["L1"])
