s("S001","For any mean-zero L^2 f, Cauchy-Schwarz in its Fourier series gives ||Dinv f||_infinity<=(sum_(k!=0)k^(-2))^(1/2)||f||_2, and coefficient weights give ||Dinv f||_(H^1)<=sqrt(2)||f||_2. For a general H^1 function its mean plus this estimate for its derivative gives ||A0||_infinity<=C||A0||_(H^1). The product rule then gives ||A0*B0||_(H^1)<=C||A0||_(H^1)||B0||_(H^1). Apply it to S_t Dinv f and S_t Dinv g and use S_t isometric and P contractive to obtain ||J_t(f,g)||_(H^1)<=C||f||_2||g||_2. Each assertion is quantified over inputs in its indicated space.",[],[],["f","g","A0","B0","t","Dinv","H","J","S","P","Fourier"],[f("F001","The inverse derivative, H^1 algebra and J_t:H^0 times H^0 -> H^1 bounds stated in N005 hold uniformly in t.",["Dinv","H","J","S"])]),
s("S002","For L^2 f,g, every Fourier coefficient of the product (S_t f)(S_t g) has modulus <=||f||_2||g||_2 by Cauchy-Schwarz in the convolution. Hence ||B_t(f,g)||_(H^(-pstar))^2<=||f||_2^2||g||_2^2 sum_(k!=0) k^2<k>^(-2*pstar), a finite sum because pstar=23/14>3/2. The same coefficient bound with sum k^(-2) proves ||Dinv P(f*g)||_2<=C||f||_2||g||_2. For mean-zero A0, <k>^(-pstar)<=|k|^(-1), so ||A0||_(H^(-pstar))<=||Dinv A0||_2. Finally J_t(A0,g) is a product of S_t Dinv A0 in L^2 and S_t Dinv g in L^infinity, followed by contractions, so its L^2 norm is <=C||Dinv A0||_2||g||_2 by F001. Approximation by polynomials and these continuous bilinear bounds justify the products and their extensions.",[],["F001"],["f","g","A0","t","B","J","Dinv","P","S","H","pstar","Fourier"],[f("F002","The B_t negative-norm estimate, the inverse-product estimate and both asymmetric negative-norm/J estimates of N005 hold.",["B","J","Dinv","H","pstar"])]),
s("S003","For the normal-form clause take smooth mean-zero paths f,g on [a,b]. At k!=0 the coefficient of J_t is sum_(r+s=k) exp(-i*t*3*k*r*s)*(i*r)^(-1)*(i*s)^(-1)*hat f(r)*hat g(s). Differentiating the phase gives 3*i*k times the product coefficients. Therefore d_t J_t(f,g)=3*B_t(f,g)+J_t(f',g)+J_t(f,g'). Smooth spatial Fourier series and all derivatives used here converge absolutely, so differentiation and integration are legitimate. Integration gives the displayed two-endpoint normal form. For piecewise smooth continuous paths apply this on each subinterval; intermediate J endpoint values cancel because the paths are continuous.",[],[],["f","g","t","a","b","J","B","Fourier"],[f("F003","The two-endpoint normal-form identity in N005 holds for smooth and continuous piecewise smooth mean-zero paths.",["f","g","a","b","J","B"])]),
s("S004","Symmetry of J is immediate from the product formula. Since Dinv partial_x=P, substitution gives J_t(B_t(f,g),h)=S_(-t)P{P[(S_t f)(S_t g)]*(S_t Dinv h)}, whose integral on I is exactly Ctri_I(f,g,h); no inverse derivative is commuted through a product. For constant f,g, integrate F003 and multiply by 1/2 to obtain Fstage_(a,t)(f,g)=(1/6)[J_s(f,g)]_a^t. This establishes every identity asserted in N005.",[],["F003"],["f","g","h","t","a","I","J","B","Ctri","Fstage","Dinv","P","S"])
