s("S003","Fix a,tau,t,w,M0 and assume L1: ||w||_(H^gamma)<=M0. Set wshift=S_a w. It is mean-zero and has the same H^gamma norm. From the definitions B_(a+r)(f,g)=S_(-a)B_r(S_a f,S_a g), hence Fstage_(a,t)(w)=S_(-a)Fstage_(0,t-a)(wshift). Apply E004 with source n=0, timestep tau, s=t-a, sigma=gamma, f=g=wshift and beta=a_gamma; take epsilon=1/8 in the middle case. All are in its interval: beta0<=a_gamma<=1+gamma in each of the three cases. The exponents are (1/2+2*gamma)/(5/2-gamma) for gamma<1/2, 11/17 for gamma=1/2, and (1+gamma)/2 for gamma>1/2. They are at least gamma: in the first case the numerator minus gamma times its denominator is 1/2-gamma/2+gamma^2>0; in the second 11/17>1/2; in the third 1>=gamma. Since tau<=1 these source powers are <=tau^gamma. Divide by c0 and use the S isometry to obtain the first stage bound.",["E004"],["F002"],["gamma","a_gamma","a","tau","t","w","wshift","M0","S","B","Fstage","H"],[f("F003","Under L1, sup_(a<=t<=a+tau)||Fstage_(a,t)(w)||_(H^(a_gamma))<=C_(gamma,M0)*tau^gamma.",["gamma","a_gamma","a","tau","w","M0","Fstage","H"],["L1"])]),
